        "verdict": "NO",
        "citation": "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/postmarket-drug-safety-information-patients-and-providers/sildenafil-marketed-viagra-and-revatio-information",
        "search_summary": "Here is a comprehensive summary of the regulatory and safety history of **sildenafil** (marketed as Viagra\u00ae for erectile dysfunction and Revatio\u00ae for pulmonary arterial hypertension):\n\n---\n\n## Has Sildenafil Been Withdrawn, Given a Black Box Warning, or Discontinued for Safety Reasons?\n\n### \u2705 Short Answer: **No full market withdrawal**, but there have been **significant safety warnings**, a **pediatric use warning**, and a **petition for a black box warning** (which was not granted).\n\n---\n\n## 1. \ud83d\udeab No Global Market Withdrawal\n\nSildenafil has **not been withdrawn from any market for safety or effectiveness reasons**. \nThe FDA Adverse Event Reporting System (FAERS) database contains decades of post-marketing safety data for sildenafil, and the drug's safety profile has been remarkably consistent with what was observed in registration trials.\n Furthermore, \npost-market surveillance data spanning over 25 years have not identified new safety signals beyond those in the original label.\n\n\nThe FDA's official list of drugs withdrawn for safety or efficacy reasons (21 CFR 216.24) does **not** include sildenafil. [See the eCFR list here](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-21/chapter-I/subchapter-C/part-216/subpart-B/section-216.24).\n\n---\n\n## 2. \u26a0\ufe0f FDA Pediatric Safety Warning (2012) \u2014 Revatio (Sildenafil for PAH in Children)\n\nThis is the most significant regulatory safety action taken against sildenafil.\n\n\nThe FDA revised the sildenafil drug label in August 2012, adding a warning stating that \"use of sildenafil, particularly chronic use, is not recommended in children.\" This recommendation was based on an observation of increasing mortality with increasing sildenafil doses in a long-term clinical trial in pediatric patients with PAH.\n\n\n\nAccording to the FDA, the purpose of the recommendation was to raise awareness of clinical trial results showing a higher risk of mortality in pediatric patients taking a high dose of sildenafil compared to those taking a low dose. However, the agency said the recommendation was not intended to suggest that sildenafil should **never** be used in children.\n\n\nThe FDA later clarified this warning. \nIn a statement announcing the clarification, the FDA said: \"The evidence behind our initial recommendation has not changed; we are simply clarifying the strength of the warning communicated in the drug label.\" The FDA further recognized \"there may be situations in which the benefit-risk profile of sildenafil may be acceptable in individual children, for example, when other treatment options are limited and sildenafil can be used with close monitoring.\"\n\n\n\ud83d\udccc Source: [FDA Drug Safety Communications \u2013 Sildenafil (Viagra and Revatio)](https://www.fda.gov/drugs/postmarket-drug-safety-information-patients-and-providers/sildenafil-marketed-viagra-and-revatio-information)\n\ud83d\udccc Source: [American College of Cardiology \u2013 FDA Issues Clarification on Sildenafil Use in Children](https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/articles/2014/04/14/11/18/fda-issues-clarification-on-sildenafil-use-in-children-with-pah)\n\n---\n\n## 3. \u26a0\ufe0f Vision Loss Warning Added to Label (2005)\n\n\nIn July 2005, the FDA updated labeling for tadalafil (Cialis), vardenafil (Levitra), and sildenafil (Viagra) to reflect a small number of post-marketing reports of sudden vision loss, while acknowledging that \"it is not possible to determine whether these oral medicines for erectile dysfunction were the cause of the loss of eyesight or whether the problem is related to other factors such as high blood pressure or diabetes.\"\n\n\nSpecifically, the condition of concern was **non-arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (NAION)**. \nWhen used to treat erectile dysfunction, NAION \u2014 a cause of decreased vision including permanent loss of vision \u2014 has been reported post-marketing in temporal association with the use of PDE-5 inhibitors, including sildenafil. Most patients had underlying anatomic or vascular risk factors for developing NAION, including low cup to disc ratio (\"crowded disc\").\n\n\n\ud83d\udccc Source: [DailyMed \u2013 Sildenafil Label (FDA)](https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=d887377e-90c5-44dc-8455-b23a6755b36f)\n\n---\n\n## 4. \ud83d\udccb Black Box Warning \u2014 Petitioned But NOT Granted\n\n\nPublic Citizen petitioned the FDA to immediately add a black box warning regarding the risks of drug-induced blindness for PDE5 inhibitors prescribed for erectile dysfunction \u2014 Viagra (sildenafil), Cialis (tadalafil), and Levitra (vardenafil). The label for Revatio, a version of sildenafil for pulmonary arterial hypertension, was also included in the recommended changes.\n\n\n\nThe petition noted that the number of cases of ischemic optic neuropathy per million prescriptions was 18-fold higher for patients taking Viagra compared with patients taking Lipitor, another drug used by people with similar risk factors.\n\n\nHowever, the FDA did **not** grant a formal black box warning for sildenafil in response to this petition. Instead, a standard warning about vision loss was added to the label (as noted above).\n\n\ud83d\udccc Source: [Public Citizen \u2013 Petition to Require a Black Box Warning for ED Drugs](https://www.citizen.org/article/petition-to-require-a-black-box-warning-for-erectile-dysfunction-drugs-sildenafil-viagra-tadalafil-cialis-and-vardenafil-levitra/)\n\ud83d\udccc Source: [PMC \u2013 Inadequate Warnings About ED Drug Blindness Risk](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC1681722/)\n\n---\n\n## 5. \ud83c\uddea\ud83c\uddfa EMA Withdrawal of \"Sildenafil FGK\" Application (2021) \u2014 Not a Safety Withdrawal\n\n\nFGK Representative Service GmbH withdrew its application for a marketing authorisation of Sildenafil FGK for the treatment of adult men with erectile dysfunction. The company withdrew the application on 19 July 2021.\n Importantly, \nSildenafil FGK was a \"hybrid medicine,\" meaning it contained the same active substance as Viagra but was to be available as a thin sublingual strip (placed under the tongue), not the standard oral tablet.\n \nThe EMA's provisional opinion was that Sildenafil FGK could not have been authorised for the treatment of erectile dysfunction, as the data submitted did not support the sublingual use.\n This was a **withdrawal of a specific novel formulation's marketing application**, not a safety withdrawal of sildenafil itself.\n\n\ud83d\udccc Source: [EMA \u2013 Sildenafil FGK Withdrawal](https://www.ema.europa.eu/en/medicines/human/EPAR/sildenafil-fgk)\n\n---\n\n## 6. \ud83d\udd14 Other Ongoing Safety Warnings (Current Label)\n\nThe current sildenafil label carries several important (non-black-box) warnings, including:\n- \nContraindication with concomitant use of organic nitrates in any form, due to the greater risk of hypotension.\n\n- \nPulmonary hypertension secondary to sickle cell disease: Sildenafil may cause serious vaso-occlusive crises.\n\n-",
        "disclosure_text": "No market-withdrawal information found in this search; this does not confirm the compound is safe."
      },
